msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:89
msgid ""
"Another common usage is to determine the language the content should be "
"served in. If you are running only a single language site, this obviously"
" isn't going to make much difference, but any site that can offer up "
"multiple translations of content will find this useful, since the browser"
" will typically send the preferred language along in the ``Accept-"
"Language`` header::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:97
msgid ""
"In this example, the browser would prefer French, with a second choice of"
" English. If your website supports English and German you would do "
"something like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:102
msgid ""
"In this example, 'en' would be returned as the current language. If no "
"match is found, it will return the first element in the ``$supported`` "
"array, so that should always be the preferred language."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:106
msgid "Strict Locale Negotiation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:110
msgid ""
"By default, locale is determined on a lossy comparison basis. So only the"
" first part of the locale string is taken into account (language). This "
"is usually sufficient. But sometimes we want to be able to distinguish "
"between regional versions such as ``en-US`` and ``en-GB`` to serve "
"different content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:114
msgid ""
"For such cases, we have introduced a new setting that can be enabled via "
"``Config\\Feature::$strictLocaleNegotiation``. This will ensure that the "
"strict comparison will be made in the first place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:119
msgid ""
"CodeIgniter comes with translations only for primary language tags ('en',"
" 'fr', etc.). So if you enable this feature and your settings in "
"``Config\\App::$supportedLocales`` include regional language tags ('en-"
"US', 'fr-FR', etc.), then keep in mind that if you have your own "
"translation files, you **must also change** the folder names for "
"CodeIgniter's translation files to match what you put in the "
"``$supportedLocales`` array."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:124
msgid ""
"Now let's consider the below example. The browser's preferred language "
"will be set as this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:129
msgid ""
"In this example, the browser would prefer French, with a second choice of"
" English (United Kingdom). Your website on another hand supports German "
"and English (United States):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:134
msgid ""
"In this example, 'en-US' would be returned as the current language. If no"
" match is found, it will return the first element in the ``$supported`` "
"array. Here is how exactly the locale selection process works."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:137
msgid ""
"Even though the 'fr' is preferred by the browser it is not in our "
"``$supported`` array. The same problem occurs with 'en-GB', but here we "
"will be able to search for variants. First, we will fallback to the most "
"general locale (in this case 'en') which again is not in our array. Then "
"we will search for the regional locale 'en-'. And that's when our value "
"from the ``$supported`` array will be matched. We will return 'en-US'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:142
msgid "So the process of selecting a locale is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:144
msgid "strict match ('en-GB') - ISO 639-1 plus ISO 3166-1 alpha-2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:145
msgid "general locale match ('en') - ISO 639-1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/incoming/content_negotiation.rst:146
msgid ""
"regional locale match ('en-') - ISO 639-1 plus \"wildcard\" for ISO "
"3166-1 alpha-2"
msgstr ""

